---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../lib/site';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy — LaunchPad"
  description="What LaunchPad stores (almost nothing), what it never does, and your rights wherever you live."
  path=""
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container column prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Legal</span>
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Short version: LaunchPad has no accounts, sets no cookies, runs no trackers, and shows no
        ads. Your browser keeps a few preferences so the site works. That is the whole story
        today, and this page will change before anything else is added.
      </p>
      <p class="updated">Last updated {site.legalUpdated}. Applies to {site.url}.</p>

      <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
      <p>
        LaunchPad is run by {site.owner}, a student in {site.ownerLocation}, as a non-commercial
        education project. For anything about your data, email
        <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a>. The same address handles
        complaints and questions from parents and teachers.
      </p>

      <h2>What we collect</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing that identifies you. There is no sign-up, no email capture, no contact form and no
        server of our own: the site is a set of static files.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your browser stores a few small preferences on your device so the site works. They never
        reach us and clearing your browser data removes them. The full list is on the
        <a href={`${base}/cookies`}>Cookies &amp; storage</a> page; the short version:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>which edition you chose, so the front page can offer it again;</li>
        <li>the currency you picked in a calculator;</li>
        <li>lessons you have finished and quiz scores;</li>
        <li>your answer to the privacy choices dialog, so we do not ask again.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The files are served by GitHub Pages. Like any web host, GitHub sees the technical
        details of each request (your IP address, browser type, the page requested) to deliver the
        page and keep the service secure. GitHub's own
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" rel="noopener noreferrer">privacy statement</a>
        covers that. We do not receive those logs.
      </p>

      <h2>What we never do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No cookies, no analytics that identify you, no advertising or social-media pixels.</li>
        <li>No selling or sharing of personal data with anyone, for any purpose.</li>
        <li>No profiling, behavioural monitoring or targeted content, and none aimed at anyone under 18.</li>
        <li>No fonts or scripts from third-party servers, so loading a page tells no one you were here.</li>
        <li>Nothing you type into a calculator leaves your browser.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>If accounts are added</h2>
      <p>
        Signing in will always be optional; the whole site works without it. If and when it
        launches, an account exists only to keep your progress in step across devices, and this
        page will be updated first. The plan, so you can hold us to it:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Collected:</strong> your email address, your progress, and the year you were born
          (kept only as "old enough" or "needs a parent"). If a parent or guardian has to agree
          first, their email address and their confirmation.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Why:</strong> to sign you in without a password and to sync your progress. Legal basis: your consent, which you can withdraw by deleting the account.</li>
        <li><strong>Passkeys:</strong> we would store only the public half of a passkey; your fingerprint or face never leaves your device.</li>
        <li><strong>Kept for:</strong> as long as you use the account, then deleted after 24 months of no sign-ins, or immediately when you ask.</li>
        <li><strong>Where:</strong> with a hosting provider we name here before launch, in a region we name too.</li>
        <li><strong>Never:</strong> public profiles, messaging between users, ads, or use of your data for anything else.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Age</h2>
      <p>
        LaunchPad is written for people aged 15 to 21. Because nothing personal is collected, you
        can use it at any age without asking anyone. If accounts launch, the rules will follow
        where you live: no accounts under 13 anywhere; a parent or guardian must agree first for
        anyone under 18 in India (Digital Personal Data Protection Act), under the local age of
        digital consent in the EU (13 to 16 depending on the country), and under 13 in the UK and
        the United States. If we learn that someone under 13 has created an account, we delete it.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Wherever you live, you can ask what we hold about you (today: nothing), ask for it to be
        corrected or deleted, and complain. Email
        <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a>; we answer within 30 days.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>European Union and United Kingdom:</strong> the rights of access, rectification,
          erasure, restriction, portability and objection under the GDPR and UK GDPR, the right to
          withdraw consent at any time, and the right to complain to your national data protection
          authority (in the UK, the ICO).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>India:</strong> the rights to access, correction, erasure and grievance redressal
          under the Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023, and to nominate someone to exercise
          them for you. Complaints can go to the Data Protection Board of India once the Act's
          provisions are in force.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>California and other US states:</strong> the rights to know, delete and correct.
          We do not sell or share personal information and never have, so there is nothing to opt
          out of; we honour the Global Privacy Control signal anyway.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Statistics, if we ever count visits</h2>
      <p>
        Any future statistics would be cookieless and aggregate-only: counts of pages read and
        calculators used, with no profile of individual visitors, no cross-site identifiers, and
        an easy way to opt out. It would be described here and switched on only after that.
      </p>

      <h2>Links and embedded content</h2>
      <p>
        Lessons link to official sources. Those sites have their own privacy policies. Any video
        from another site loads only after you tap it and only if you have allowed it in Privacy
        choices; until then, nothing is requested from that site.
      </p>

      <h2>Security</h2>
      <p>
        The site is served over HTTPS only. Because no personal data is held, there is nothing to
        breach today; if accounts launch, we will publish how the data is protected and how we
        would notify you if something went wrong. Security researchers can find a contact in our
        <a href={`${base}/.well-known/security.txt`}>security.txt</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        When this page changes, the date at the top changes with it, and the full history is in
        the public <a href={site.repo} rel="noopener noreferrer">source repository</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
